From bd6f852cc68e098d182fe08b70dbdccb38aef637 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kernel-wasm <kernel-wasm@localhost>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 22:33:16 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] wasm: run x86-64 user space

kwemu's guest CPU can be x86-64 (include/kw_emu.h with
KW_USER_ISA_X86_64). Present the x86-64 Linux user ABI, selected at build
time with CONFIG_WASM_USER_X86_64 (AArch64 stays the default):

- syscalls: rax = number/return, rdi rsi rdx r10 r8 r9 = arguments,
  arch/x86/entry/syscalls/syscall_64.tbl without x32, the x86 clone order
  (no CLONE_BACKWARDS), arch_prctl for FS/GS bases, and the selector and
  port syscalls as the errors a real kernel gives;
- ELF: EM_X86_64, AT_PLATFORM "x86_64", x86-64 CPUID EDX as AT_HWCAP,
  uname -m x86_64; /proc/cpuinfo in x86's format;
- process: child rax = 0, CLONE_SETTLS sets the FS base, exec starts with
  RFLAGS.IF and the flat user segments, and show_regs dumps the x86 GPRs;
- signals: x86-64's rt_sigframe (pretcode with SA_RESTORER, ucontext,
  siginfo and an FXSAVE area), sigreturn restores the GPRs, RIP/RSP,
  RFLAGS, FS/GS bases, MXCSR and XMM registers, and syscall restart
  rewinds by the two SYSCALL bytes;
- page tables stay Sv48 and TASK_SIZE becomes the 47-bit user range;
- ptrace regsets, core dumps, seccomp filters and the sigpage remain
  arm64's (the x86 layouts are the next step).

A static x86-64 init reaches user mode, calls arch_prctl and readlink,
and powers the kernel off.
---
